WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:11.667
This is the lesson's lab: the two rows are pos and neg, starting from the instructor's example ABCED / DABCE.

2
00:00:11.667 --> 00:00:20.467
The result is judged item by item: complete, no overlap and fits are separate. This pair gives 9 × 4, and it fits.

3
00:00:20.467 --> 00:00:28.667
Back to the start and step: A comes before B in both sequences, so A is left of B, giving the H edge A→B.

4
00:00:28.667 --> 00:00:37.200
Three more steps to A and D: A is first in pos but D is first in neg; the lines cross, A is below D, a V edge.

5
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:46.133
Jump to the end and click block E: x[E] = 7, set by C. Of its three horizontal predecessors, the largest bound wins.

6
00:00:46.133 --> 00:00:57.000
Switch to the preset “valid but overflows vertically”: ABCDE / EDCBA, every pair is above/below, a 3 × 10 stack. No overlap, but it does not fit.

7
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:07.200
Back to the instructor's example: select D in pos and move it one place left, ABCDE / DABCE. The relation changed, the packing did not.

8
00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:15.200
“Random neighbour” is the basic annealing move: swap two blocks at random. Undo if you do not like it.

9
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:24.667
The code lab: your code runs in the browser, with no Python to install. pack() and trace() are written; five tasks are left.

10
00:01:24.667 --> 00:01:30.800
Run the checks first: nothing is written yet, so the result is INCOMPLETE.

11
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:40.000
Task 2: where a sits relative to b. The same order in both sequences means left/right; opposite orders mean below/above.

12
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:56.894
Task 4: the longest path. In topological order, take the largest “predecessor coordinate + predecessor size”, and record who set it.

13
00:01:56.894 --> 00:02:07.627
For this demo, tasks 1, 3 and 5 (validate, build the graphs, metrics) are filled in from the reference; then run the checks: everything passes.

14
00:02:07.627 --> 00:02:18.694
Below, your functions run on eight cases, compared block by block with the course engine: coordinates, the chosen predecessor, fit and bounding box.

15
00:02:18.694 --> 00:02:26.761
Click the “three blocks” row: B starts at x = 3; your code on the left, the course engine on the right.

16
00:02:26.761 --> 00:02:33.894
Finally, try the challenges to check you really understand. Results stay in your browser.
